  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 218 AM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nJust when you think it couldn't get any hotter, we're staring into\nthe face of what looks to be the hottest day of the year today.  \nThe heart of both the upper level ridge and mid level thermal \nridge will be centered across the Front Range. 700-600 mb \ntemperatures warm another 0.5-1.0C, which means we'll tack on \nanother degree or two to yesterday's highs that exceeded 100F \nover most of the plains and I-25 Corridor. That means record \ntemperatures are forecast for many stations, with a sampling \nbelow;\n\nCity\t\tForecast\t\tOld Record\n\nDenver: \t   102\t\t\t100 in 1910\nFort Collins:  102\t\t\t100 in 1910\nBoulder:\t   101\t\t\t 98 in 1935\nGreeley UNC:   105\t\t\t102 in 2003\nFort Morgan:   106\t\t\t106 in 1910\nAkron:\t\t   101\t\t\t104 in 2003\nJulesburg:\t   105\t\t\t105 in 1894\nLimon: \t\t   100\t \t\t 98 in 2023\n\nGiven a further uptick in temperatures, we've expanded the Heat \nAdvisory to also include the Palmer Divide where more significant\nheat impact levels are forecast. Warm overnight lows, and in some\nplaces record high minimums, are only further intensifying the \nheat impacts today. \n\nWith regard to any storm chances, the mid level water vapor loop \nand latest ACARS soundings show similar to just a slight uptick in\nmoisture values across the area. Therefore, we expect a similar \nday to yesterday when we saw scattered showers and a few storms in\nthe afternoon, with the highest coverage over the mountains and \nPalmer Divide, and lower chances as we head to the east with \nblazing sunshine expected through the afternoon. At least there \nwill be a little heat relief due to clouds and shower outflows \nin/near the mountains late in the day. \n\nFor tonight, it'll be another warm one as cloud cover is expected\nto increase with a push in mid/upper level monsoonal moisture. \nSome areas near the lower foothills and Denver metro area may only\nreach the mid 70s for overnight lows. Fortunately, it appears the\nclouds will help limit heating for Monday, with some of the short\nterm guidance showing sufficient cloudiness to keep us away from \nanother day of Heat Advisories. The clouds may also impact \nconvective development, but typically enough heating occurs to get\nlate day showers and storms going. This time, they may linger \nwell into the overnight hours Monday night which would be more \ntypical of a monsoonal moisture surge. As precipitable water (PW)\nvalues increase to 150-200% of normal, some of the storms will be\ncapable of producing locally heavy rainfall.\n\nThat threat may very well grow into Tuesday as we'll be approaching\nclimatological maxes for PW. At the same time, warm cloud depths \nincrease a little to 3,000-4,000 feet but that's still less than\noptimal given the very warm airmass and still deep subcloud \nlayer. All other parameters are more favorable including slow \nstorm motion (15 mph or less), high PW, and potential for skinny \nCAPE profiles above the LCL. Thus, we'll continue to watch for \nlocally heavy rainfall and any flash flood threat. Wednesday could\nbe another similar setup with scattered to numerous showers and \nstorms with locally heavy rainfall, although storm motions will \nlikely increase as the ridge flattens and a shortwave moves \nthrough. Temperatures are expected to settle closer to normal \n(still above, though) as we head into this more unsettled pattern \nand a couple backdoor \"cool\" fronts are expected. \n\nAfter that, the upper level ridge is still forecast to retrograde\ninto the Great Basin late next week through next weekend. This\nwould start to advect in drier air in the mid/upper levels in NW\nflow aloft. At the same time, low level moisture could still hold  \neast of the Rockies. We do think overall coverage of afternoon/ \nevening showers and storms will be on the decrease, which means \ntemperatures will likely be on the rise by next weekend.",
